{\pard\sb360\sa120\keepn\b 2-author/ \u183? A2 to A4, what the author sends\par}
{\pard\sa160 One folder per paper: the research record, kept by the author (and the author's AI) as the work proceeds. The layout is the protocol's ({\f1 ../1-journal/protocol/formal.md#P1}):\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 record.yaml          manifest\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 declarations.yaml    modality, standard version, access tier, AI use\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 paper/               for people\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 appendix/            for the reader who checks; ends with the scope-and-choices section\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 ai-appendix/         generated: definitions, procedure, claims, stance, anchors, scope, and the stamp\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 materials/           code that reproduces reported values\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 provenance/          derivations.yaml (the derivation behind each proposition) and ai-use.yaml\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 self-check/<run>/    A3: the author's own runs of the published checks\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 submission.yaml      A4: written by `mark1 submit`\par}
{\pard\sa160\par}
{\pard\sa160 The author sends the record itself, nothing else. {\f1 mark1 submit} copies it as received to {\f1 ../3-editor/<paper>/rounds/r<n>/}, and the journal works on that copy from then on; the author keeps working here.\par}
{\pard\sa160 A synthetic record, {\f1 toy-monopoly/}, lived here until 2026-10-22; it was removed at Kalanoski's request. A labelled simulation of the later steps, {\f1 questions-before-priors-simulation/}, was removed on 2026-10-28: the demonstration stops at round 2's intake, and the tests take a copy of the record the rest of the way.\par}
{\pard\sa160 A real paper is added as a new folder. Source material stays outside the repository; the paper and its appendix are extracted into a record here, and anything confidential stays behind. For a paper that exists as LaTeX, {\f1 mark1 import-tex <file.tex> <paper-id> --choices <label> --does-not-claim <label>} writes {\f1 paper/} and {\f1 appendix/} in the protocol's form; the author then adds {\f1 record.yaml}, {\f1 declarations.yaml}, {\f1 materials/} and {\f1 provenance/}, and reads what the converter wrote.\par}
{\pard\sa160 {\f1 questions-before-priors/} is the first real paper: Ryall's {\i Questions Before Priors}, a theorem-proof paper built from the repository dropped on 2026-10-21. Its {\f1 README.md} says what came from the source, what Claude Code wrote, and what the author has not yet seen. It brought protocol formal-0.2 with it: derivations of kind {\f1 proof}, because none of its results is a closed form the machine can re-solve.\par}
